This is a short scratch computation and is mathematically elementary. It is unrelated to the homotopy-theoretic scribbles on the previous page (Postnikov invariant k_1 of a loop space X = ΩY), and apparently also to the Gauss–Manin / Picard–Fuchs material of the dossier.

For a function f of a parameter t, the logarithmic differential df/f is squared to give a quadratic differential: (df/f)^2 = (df)^2/f^2 = (f'_t/f)^2 dt^2. Squaring makes the object insensitive to the sign of f (and of df), so it is a well-defined quadratic differential.

Grothendieck then lists properties a), b), d), e) (with c) struck out), which are not written out. He defines, for two functions f and g,
E(f,g) = (dg/g)^2 / (df/f)^2 = (dg/df)^2 / (g/f)^2,
the ratio of two quadratic differentials. This is a function, namely the square of the logarithmic derivative (the 'elasticity') d log g / d log f = (f/g)(dg/df).

The properties a)–e) are presumably the obvious ones:
- invariance under the scalings f -> cf and g -> cg;
- E(f,f) = 1;
- the chain rule E(f,h) = E(f,g)E(g,h);
- E(f,g^n) = n^2 E(f,g);
- independence of the parameter t.

A crossed-out scribble 'd/dx G_a(c)' (or similar) at the bottom is illegible and abandoned.
